Snippet (lines 13-17):
```
    fread (data, 2, 2, ifp);
    tag =  data[0] << 8 | data[1];
    len = (data[2] << 8 | data[3]) - 2;
    if (tag <= 0xff00) return 0;
    fread (data, 1, len, ifp);
```

Step 2 – Branch (tag guard misplaced / incomplete): Condition `if (tag <= 0xff00) return 0;` at line 16. For attacker-controlled input where `tag > 0xff00`, false branch executes, skipping return. The guard does not validate `len`, does not verify `len < sizeof(data)`, and is placed before the dangerous `fread` but without the needed length check, so it fails to protect the buffer.
Snippet (lines 14-17): same as above.

Step 3 – Loop (do-while with untrusted bound): `do { ... } while (tag != 0xffda);` at lines 12-40. Loop has no numeric bound; termination depends on file-derived `tag`. Each iteration recomputes `len` directly from `data[2..3]` (line 15) and performs unsized `fread` (line 17). No bounds check verifies `len <= sizeof(data)-2` before array write or before case accesses within the loop.
Snippet (lines 12-17):
```
  do {
    fread (data, 2, 2, ifp);
    ...
    fread (data, 1, len, ifp);
    ...
  } while (tag != 0xffda);
```

Step 4 – Sequence (unbounded array access inside cases): Case `0xffc3` (line 19) accesses `data[7]` (line 20) unconditionally; if `len < 8`, out-of-bounds. Case `0xffc4` (line 28) loop `for (dp = data; dp < data+len ...)` (line 30) uses attacker-derived `len`; with underflow, reads/writes past buffer via `make_decoder_ref`. Case `0xffda` (line 33) uses `data[0]` as index multiplier (`data[1+data[0]*2]`, line 34) with no range/length check.
Snippet (lines 19-20):
```
      case 0xffc3:
	jh->sraw = ((data[7] >> 4) * (data[7] & 15) - 1) & 3;
```
Snippet (lines 30-31):
```
	for (dp = data; dp < data+len && (c = *dp++) < 4; )
	  jh->free[c] = jh->huff[c] = make_decoder_ref (&dp);
```
Snippet (lines 33-35):
```
      case 0xffda:
	jh->psv = data[1+data[0]*2];
	jh->bits -= data[3+data[0]*2] & 15;
```

